# What the console image's build context may contain.
#
# The console is built from the repository ROOT
# (`docker-compose.yml`'s `console` service, and the staging deploy's
# `docker build -f Dockerfile.frontend .`), so this file decides what
# that build sees. It applies to a build whose context is the repository
# root only — the API image's context is `./rust` and the Dagster
# image's is `./dagster`, and neither reads this file (Docker looks for
# `.dockerignore` at the root of the context it was given).
#
# What the frontend build MUST see, and therefore what this file must
# not exclude: `package.json`, `bun.lock`, `bunfig.toml`, `src/` (apart
# from the Lakehouse site, below), `public/`,
# `design-system/` (the `@rantai/design-system` path alias in
# `tsconfig.json`), `design-kit/`, `GTM/`, `next.config.ts`,
# `tsconfig.json`, `postcss.config.mjs`, `eslint.config.mjs`,
# `components.json`, `happydom.ts`, `testing-cleanup.ts`, and
# `rust/crates/lakehouse-api/tests/fixtures/` (a fixture the frontend's
# own type check imports — see below).
#
# Everything below is a secret, a build artifact, or a part of the
# product this image does not serve.

# Secrets and local settings. `.env` holds generated credentials
# (POSTGRES_PASSWORD, RUSTFS keys, the encryption key); baking it into
# an image would publish them to whoever pulls that image.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Dependencies and build artifacts of the host checkout.
node_modules
.next
next-env.d.ts
*.tsbuildinfo

# The git history and the CI configuration: neither is part of the
# console.
.git
.github

# The parts of the repository this image does not serve.
#
# `rust/` is NOT excluded: the frontend's own type check reads a fixture
# the API's tests own (`src/lib/connectors/secret-reference.test.ts`
# imports `rust/crates/lakehouse-api/tests/fixtures/secret_manager_refs.json`),
# so the build fails without it. Only the API's build output is excluded —
# it is gigabytes, and it is never part of an image.
rust/target
dagster
docs
ops
scripts

# The Lakehouse site (WEB-5, `site/`), the product page that was the
# console's `/landing` (WEB-1), is not served by an installed Lakehouse:
# the product owner decided on 2026-11-02 that it is a separate product
# page and that an install must not offer it (WEB-2). It is its own
# package, built as a static export and hosted on its own, so no console
# image needs it, and keeping it out of the build context means no
# console image can carry it. `/landing` answers with the console's
# page-not-found.
site/
